For methods that return `CompletableFuture`, testing is straightforward: call the method, get the result with a timeout.

```java
@Test
void shouldFetchUserAsync() {
    CompletableFuture<User> future = userService.findByIdAsync(1L);

    User result = assertTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5), () -> future.get());

    assertThat(result.getName()).isEqualTo("John");
}

@Test
void shouldHandleAsyncFailure() {
    CompletableFuture<User> future = userService.findByIdAsync(999L);

    assertThatThrownBy(() -> future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS))
        .hasCauseInstanceOf(UserNotFoundException.class);
}
```

JUnit 5's `assertTimeout()` fails with a clear message if the future doesn't complete in time. For exception testing, `CompletableFuture.get()` wraps the original exception in `ExecutionException` — use `.hasCauseInstanceOf()` to assert on the actual cause.

For `CompletableFuture` chains (`thenApply`, `thenCompose`, `exceptionally`), test the final result, not intermediate stages. The chain's correctness is proven by the output, not by verifying each transformation step.

Methods annotated with `@Async` that return `void` are the hardest to test — there's no future to wait on. The test must observe a side effect (database write, event publish) to know the async work completed.

```java
@SpringBootTest
class NotificationServiceTest {
    @Autowired NotificationService notificationService;
    @Autowired NotificationRepository notificationRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldSendNotificationAsync() {
        notificationService.sendAsync(new NotificationRequest("user@test.com", "Hello"));

        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
               .untilAsserted(() -> {
                   List<Notification> sent = notificationRepository.findByEmail("user@test.com");
                   assertThat(sent).hasSize(1);
                   assertThat(sent.get(0).getMessage()).isEqualTo("Hello");
               });
    }
}
```

Awaitility's `await().atMost().untilAsserted()` polls the assertion repeatedly until it passes or the timeout expires. This adapts to actual execution speed — fast locally, tolerant of CI slowness.

Note: `@Async` requires `@EnableAsync` and a Spring context to work — you can't test it with plain Mockito. The `@SpringBootTest` is necessary because the async proxy is created by Spring's post-processor. Verify that your test configuration includes `@EnableAsync`, otherwise the method runs synchronously and the test passes for the wrong reason.

For testing `CompletableFuture.allOf()` or `anyOf()` compositions, use `assertTimeout()` on the composed future. Test both the success path (all futures complete) and failure path (one future fails and the composition handles it). This verifies that your composition logic propagates exceptions correctly and doesn't silently swallow failures.
